   "text": "I read the structure as a cyclic peptide and traced it from the C-terminal glycinamide around the macrocycle.\n\n**Sequence, in the order I traced it:**\n1. Glycinamide, then Cys. The Cys sulfur carries a CH2C(=O) group (thioether), which acylates the Tyr nitrogen.\n2. Tyr, then N-Me-Ala, then Asn, then Pro.\n3. Dap (its side-chain NH is one end of the cyclic bridge), then Val, then 4-hydroxy-Pro (trans, 2S,4R), then Trp.\n4. A diamino acid with a two-carbon side chain (Dab). Its side-chain NH is the other end of the bridge.\n5. N1-carboxymethyl-Trp, then N-Me-Nle, then N-Me-Nle, then Leu. The Leu carbonyl closes the ring onto the Cys NH.\n\n**Bridge between the Dab and Dap side chains:** the drawing shows Dab\u2013NH\u2013CH2\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH2\u2013NH\u2013Dap. The double bond is drawn cis, so I wrote it as Z.\n\n**Ambiguities in the drawing:**\n- The long bond from the top NH down to the Dab chain could hide an extra carbon; I read it as a single bond, giving a two-carbon Dab side chain.\n- The wedge/hash on the Val \u03b1-carbon is hard to read. I took it as hashed, which gives L-Val; a wedge would make it D.\n\n**Stereochemistry:** I assigned each centre from the drawn wedges and hashes. All residues come out L (S, except Cys, which is R as in L-Cys), and the hydroxyproline comes out 2S,4R.\n\nSMILES: NC(=O)CNC(=O)[C@H](CSCC(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1ccc(O)cc1)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](C)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(N)=O)C(=O)N2CCC[C@H]2C(=O)N[C@@H](CN%10)C(=O)N[C@@H](C(C)C)C(=O)N3C[C@H](O)C[C@H]3C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc4c[nH]c5ccccc45)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCNC/C=C\\C%10)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc6cn(CC(=O)O)c7ccccc67)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](CCCC)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](CCCC)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(C)C)C9=O)N9"
